    "insert": "\nThe exposé argues that holographic AI assistants—a fusion of augmented-reality visualization and AI reasoning—could dramatically improve operational efficiency, decision-making, collaboration, and creativity in professional settings such as healthcare, education, and engineering. To explore this promise, the author frames one overarching research question and five sub-questions that probe training requirements, productivity gains, enabling technologies, adoption barriers, and creative potential. The study will use a source-based literature review guided by the Task-Technology Fit (TTF) framework to synthesize existing evidence. Anticipated outcomes include case studies, best-practice recommendations, and strategies for overcoming cost, training, and standardization hurdles, despite limitations tied to reliance on secondary data.\n\n"
